learnings:
  - 'The keystone worked exactly as the audit predicted: ONE schema change closed two Aegis criticals (C1 chain + C2 binding), added agent attribution for the moat, and set up the DAG for branching — and doing it before M1c means the store becomes authoritative on an authenticated ledger, not a forgeable one.'
  - 'Sequencing paid for itself immediately: building the migration SURFACED a pre-existing bug (the #grade in-place mutation of a hashed field, unverifiable seq 1–7) that a naive M1c-first path would have cemented as authoritative. The verify tool now proves the real fabric self-verifies (exit 0) — a capability that literally did not exist before this session.'
  - 'Guardrail discipline held: told Builder to hard-stop on the v1-recompute check; it did, correctly diagnosed pre-existing-vs-mine on a pristine tree, and we ruled before writing more code. Then independent re-run caught 2 non-hermetic tests (reading the live growing fabric) that Builder''s own run missed — fixed by freezing the fixture to the v1 prefix. Always re-run the suite yourself; a subagent''s green can be stale.'
